                <head>No. 1433<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">Editorial Note</hi></head>
                <p>In early February 1952, the Saudi Arabian Embassy in Washington requested the
                    White House to send a medical mission to Saudi Arabia because of the illness of
                    King Ibn Saud. Major General Wallace H.
                    Graham, personal physician to President Truman, was in charge of the mission, which left Washington on
                    February 15 and returned the end of February. Regarding previous medical
                    missions to Saudi Arabia by General Graham, see telegram 203, April 8, 1950, and
                    the memorandum by Frederick H. Awalt,
                    April 30, 1950, <ref target="frus:frus1950v05/pg_1157"><hi rendition="#italic"
                            >Foreign Relations</hi>, 1950, volume <hi rendition="#small-caps"
                        >v</hi>, pages 1157</ref> and <ref target="frus:frus1950v05/pg_1169"
                        >1169</ref>; and the memorandum by Awalt, August 14, 1951, <ref target="frus:frus1951v05/pg_1066"
                            ><hi rendition="#italic">ibid</hi>., 1951, volume <hi
                            rendition="#small-caps">v</hi>, page 1066</ref>. Documentation on the
                    1952 trip is in Department of State file 786A.11.</p>
            </div>
